<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eldred Pottinger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1811–1843</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eldred Pottinger was an officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> Artillery who, travelling in disguise, was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> besieged it in 1837–1838 and helped organise its defence. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war">First Anglo-Afghan War</a> he served as political agent in Kohistan and survived the retreat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in 1842 as a hostage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> in 1843.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eldred-pottinger.jpg" alt="Eldred Pottinger" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Print made by: Lowes Cato Dickinson After: Sir Vincent Eyre · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prison_Sketches._Comprising_portraits_of_the_Cabul_prisoners,_and_other_subjects_(BM_1970,0527.2.10).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
